var CURR = {
"Mathematics": [
 ["Sets & Venn Diagrams", ["set","venn","union","intersection","subset","element"],
  "A set is a well-defined collection of objects called elements. The union (A∪B) contains every element in A or B; the intersection (A∩B) contains only elements in both. Two sets are disjoint when their intersection is empty. Venn diagrams show these relationships as overlapping circles and are used to solve real counting problems.",
  [{"q":"If A = {2,4,6} and B = {4,5,6}, find A∩B.","o":["{2,4}","{4,6}","{2,4,5,6}","{5,6}"],"a":1,"e":"The intersection contains elements present in both sets: 4 and 6."},
   {"q":"In a class of 30, 18 play football and 14 play basketball, while 6 play both. How many play neither?","o":["2","4","6","8"],"a":1,"e":"18+14−6=26 play at least one; 30−26=4 play neither."}]],
 ["Logarithms & Indices", ["logarithm","indices","base","exponent","law","equation"],
  "Indices express repeated multiplication: a^m × a^n = a^(m+n) and a^m ÷ a^n = a^(m−n), with a^0 = 1. Logarithms are the inverse of indices: if a^x = N then log_a N = x. The three log laws are log(xy) = log x + log y, log(x÷y) = log x − log y and log x^n = n log x. Common logarithms use base 10.",
  [{"q":"Simplify 2^3 × 2^4.","o":["2^7","2^12","4^7","2^1"],"a":0,"e":"Add the indices: 2^(3+4) = 2^7."},
   {"q":"Given log 2 = 0.301, find log 8.","o":["0.602","0.903","1.204","0.753"],"a":1,"e":"log 8 = log 2^3 = 3 × 0.301 = 0.903."}]],
 ["Matrices", ["matrix","determinant","inverse","identity","row","column","order"],
  "A matrix is a rectangular array of numbers arranged in rows and columns; its order is rows × columns. Matrices of the same order can be added or subtracted element by element. Two matrices multiply when the columns of the first equal the rows of the second. For a 2×2 matrix, the determinant is ad − bc, and the matrix has an inverse only when the determinant is not zero.",
  [{"q":"Find the determinant of the matrix [[2,3],[1,4]].","o":["5","11","8","−5"],"a":0,"e":"det = (2×4) − (3×1) = 8 − 3 = 5."},
   {"q":"For a 2×2 matrix to have an inverse, its determinant must be…","o":["zero","one","non-zero","negative"],"a":2,"e":"A matrix whose determinant is zero is singular and has no inverse."}]],
 ["Bearings & Distances", ["bearing","orientation","compass","sine rule","cosine rule","direction"],
  "A bearing is the clockwise angle from north, written as three figures (040°, 135°). North and south bearings are opposite directions: the bearing of the point south is 180°. Distances between points are found with the sine and cosine rules when the triangle is not right-angled: a/sinA = b/sinB = c/sinC and a² = b² + c² − 2bc cosA.",
  [{"q":"The bearing of Q from P is 060°. What is the bearing of P from Q?","o":["120°","240°","300°","60°"],"a":1,"e":"Add 180° to the back bearing: 60° + 180° = 240°."},
   {"q":"In triangle PQR, PQ = 8 km, PR = 10 km and angle P = 60°. Find QR using the cosine rule.","o":["≈9.2 km","≈12.8 km","≈10.5 km","≈15.2 km"],"a":0,"e":"QR² = 8²+10²−2(8)(10)cos60° = 84, so QR ≈ 9.2 km."}]],
 ["Sequences & Series (AP/GP)", ["arithmetic","geometric","common difference","common ratio","nth term","sum"],
  "An arithmetic progression (AP) changes by a constant common difference d: Tn = a + (n−1)d. A geometric progression (GP) multiplies by a constant common ratio r: Tn = ar^(n−1). The sum of n terms of an AP is Sn = n/2 × (2a + (n−1)d); the sum of a GP is Sn = a(r^n − 1)/(r − 1) when r ≠ 1.",
  [{"q":"Find the 10th term of the AP 3, 7, 11, 15,…","o":["39","43","35","47"],"a":0,"e":"a=3, d=4; T10 = 3 + (10−1)(4) = 39."},
   {"q":"Find the sum of the first 5 terms of the GP 2, 6, 18,…","o":["242","360","121","182"],"a":0,"e":"a=2, r=3; S5 = 2(3^5−1)/(3−1) = 242."}]],
 ["Trigonometry: Sine & Cosine Rules", ["sine rule","cosine rule","trigonometry","triangle","angle","side"],
  "The sine rule relates sides to opposite angles in any triangle: a/sinA = b/sinB = c/sinC. The cosine rule finds a side from two sides and the included angle: a² = b² + c² − 2bc cosA. The area of any triangle is ½ab sinC. These rules work in non-right-angled triangles, which appear frequently in bearings and height-and-distance problems.",
  [{"q":"In triangle ABC, a = 10, b = 12 and angle B = 50°. Using the sine rule, sinA = ?","o":["(10 sin50°)/12","(12 sin50°)/10","(10 sin40°)/12","5/6"],"a":0,"e":"sinA/a = sinB/b gives sinA = a sinB / b = (10 sin50°)/12."},
   {"q":"The area of a triangle with sides 6 and 8 enclosing an angle of 30° is…","o":["12","24","48","10"],"a":0,"e":"Area = ½ × 6 × 8 × sin30° = 24 × 0.5 = 12."}]]
],
"English Language": [
 ["Letter Writing (Formal & Informal)", ["letter","formal","informal","salutation","address","signature"],
  "Letters have three fixed parts: the writer's address and date, the salutation, and the body, closing with a signature. Formal letters begin Dear Sir/Madam and end Yours faithfully; informal letters use the recipient's first name and end Yours sincerely or With love. Formal letters must state the purpose clearly in the first paragraph and keep a polite, businesslike tone throughout.",
  [{"q":"Which ending matches a letter that opens Dear Sir?","o":["Yours sincerely","Yours faithfully","With love","Best wishes"],"a":1,"e":"Dear Sir/Dear Madam pairs with Yours faithfully."},
   {"q":"The address of the writer in a formal letter appears…","o":["at the end","only in the body","at the top right","after the signature"],"a":2,"e":"The writer's address and date go at the top, before the salutation."}]],
 ["Essay Writing (Narrative & Argumentative)", ["essay","narrative","argumentative","introduction","conclusion","paragraph"],
  "A good essay has an introduction that states the topic, a body of two or more developed paragraphs, and a conclusion that rounds off the argument. Narrative essays tell a story in clear chronological order with vivid details. Argumentative essays take a clear stand, give three or more supporting points with examples, and answer the opposing side politely.",
  [{"q":"Which sentence best begins an argumentative essay on examination malpractice?","o":["Once upon a time there was a school","Examination malpractice destroys the worth of every certificate it touches","I woke up and went to school","There are many students in Nigeria"],"a":1,"e":"An argumentative essay opens with the writer's stand, not a story or a general description."},{"q":"Which paragraph of an argumentative essay restates the main stand?","o":["the introduction","the first body paragraph","the conclusion","the title"],"a":2,"e":"The conclusion summarises and restates the position taken in the essay."}]],
 ["Summary Writing", ["summary","main point","concise","topic sentence","own words","key idea"],
  "A summary restates the main ideas of a passage in your own words and in a fixed number of sentences or words. Read the passage twice, identify the topic of each paragraph, then write one sentence per main idea, dropping examples, repetitions and minor details. Marks go to content, conciseness and the use of your own words — never copy whole sentences.",
  [{"q":"The first step in summary writing is to…","o":["copy the first sentence","read the passage and find the main ideas","count the words","rewrite the passage in full"],"a":1,"e":"Identify the main ideas of each paragraph before writing your own version."},{"q":"Which of these must a good summary avoid?","o":["your own wording","copied sentences","the main ideas","a fixed length"],"a":1,"e":"Summaries are written in your own words; copying loses marks."}]],
 ["Idioms & Phrasal Verbs", ["idiom","phrasal verb","figurative","meaning","expression"],
  "An idiom is a fixed expression whose meaning is figurative, not literal: to let the cat out of the bag means to reveal a secret. A phrasal verb is a verb plus a particle: give up (surrender), look after (care for), put off (postpone). The same verb takes different meanings with different particles, so learn them in context, not in isolation.",
  [{"q":"To hit the nail on the head means to…","o":["fail badly","say exactly the right thing","work very hard","be very angry"],"a":1,"e":"The idiom means to describe a situation precisely."},
   {"q":"She has decided to give up smoking. Give up here means…","o":["to begin","to stop","to sell","to forget"],"a":1,"e":"Give up = stop doing something."}]],
 ["Tenses & Concord", ["tense","present","past","future","concord","agreement","subject"],
  "Tense marks the time of an action: present (works), past (worked), future (will work), with progressive (is working) and perfect (has worked) forms. Concord means the subject and verb agree: singular subjects take singular verbs (The boy runs) and plural subjects take plural verbs (The boys run). Intervening phrases do not change the agreement.",
  [{"q":"Choose the correct sentence.","o":["The list of books are on the table","The list of books is on the table","The lists of books is on the table","The list of book are on table"],"a":1,"e":"The subject is the singular noun list, so the verb must be singular (is)."},{"q":"Neither the teacher nor the students ___ in the hall.","o":["is","are","was","has"],"a":1,"e":"With neither…nor the verb agrees with the nearer subject: the students → are."}]],
 ["Figures of Speech", ["simile","metaphor","personification","hyperbole","alliteration","imagery"],
  "Figures of speech give language force and colour. A simile compares using like or as (brave as a lion); a metaphor compares directly (time is money); personification gives human qualities to things (the wind whispered); hyperbole is deliberate exaggeration; alliteration repeats initial sounds; and imagery appeals to the senses. Identifying them is a regular WAEC/NECO objective.",
  [{"q":"The wind whispered through the trees is an example of…","o":["simile","personification","hyperbole","alliteration"],"a":1,"e":"The wind is given the human action of whispering."},
   {"q":"His smile is a ray of sunshine is an example of…","o":["metaphor","simile","personification","onomatopoeia"],"a":0,"e":"It compares directly without like or as, so it is a metaphor."}]]
],
"Biology": [
 ["Cell Division: Mitosis & Meiosis", ["mitosis","meiosis","chromosome","cell division","daughter cell","nucleus"],
  "Cell division produces new cells. Mitosis produces two daughter cells identical to the parent (same chromosome number) and drives growth and repair. Meiosis produces four cells with half the chromosome number and is the basis of gamete formation and variation. Both begin with chromosome duplication, but meiosis involves two divisions and crossing-over.",
  [{"q":"How many daughter cells are formed from one cell by meiosis?","o":["2","4","8","3"],"a":1,"e":"Meiosis produces four cells, each with half the chromosome number."},
   {"q":"Mitosis is mainly responsible for…","o":["gamete formation","growth and repair","crossing-over","halving chromosome number"],"a":1,"e":"Mitosis produces identical cells for growth and repair."}]],
 ["Nutrition in Plants & Animals", ["photosynthesis","respiration","nutrition","chlorophyll","digestion","glucose"],
  "Photosynthesis makes glucose from carbon dioxide and water using light energy trapped by chlorophyll, releasing oxygen. Animal nutrition is heterotrophic: food is ingested, digested, absorbed and assimilated. Enzymes speed up digestion — amylase breaks starch to maltose, protease breaks protein to amino acids, and lipase breaks fats to fatty acids and glycerol.",
  [{"q":"The site of photosynthesis in a plant cell is the…","o":["nucleus","chloroplast","vacuole","cell wall"],"a":1,"e":"Chloroplasts contain chlorophyll and carry out photosynthesis."},
   {"q":"Amylase digests starch into…","o":["amino acids","fatty acids","maltose","glucose only"],"a":2,"e":"Amylase breaks starch down into maltose, which maltase then turns to glucose."}]],
 ["The Respiratory System", ["respiration","lungs","gaseous exchange","alveoli","diaphragm","oxygen"],
  "Respiration releases energy from food, and can be aerobic (with oxygen) or anaerobic (without). In humans, air passes through the trachea and bronchi to the alveoli, where gaseous exchange occurs: oxygen diffuses into the blood and carbon dioxide out. The alveoli are adapted by being moist, thin-walled and richly supplied with capillaries.",
  [{"q":"Gaseous exchange in the lungs takes place in the…","o":["bronchi","trachea","alveoli","diaphragm"],"a":2,"e":"Alveoli are the tiny air sacs where oxygen and carbon dioxide are exchanged."},{"q":"Air enters the lungs through the…","o":["oesophagus","trachea","spine","aorta"],"a":1,"e":"The trachea (windpipe) carries air to the bronchi and lungs."}]],
 ["Coordination: The Nervous System", ["neurone","reflex","brain","spinal cord","impulse","synapse"],
  "Coordination is carried out by the nervous and endocrine systems. A neurone carries electrical impulses; the CNS (brain and spinal cord) receives and processes them; a reflex action is a fast, automatic response that does not involve conscious thought, such as withdrawing a hand from a flame. Impulses cross the synapse between neurones by chemicals called neurotransmitters.",
  [{"q":"A reflex action is best described as a response that is…","o":["conscious and slow","automatic and fast","learned and deliberate","voluntary and slow"],"a":1,"e":"Reflexes are automatic, rapid responses that protect the body."},{"q":"The basic unit of the nervous system is the…","o":["neurone","hormone","alveolus","nephron"],"a":0,"e":"Neurones (nerve cells) carry impulses."}]],
 ["Reproduction in Humans", ["gamete","fertilisation","ovulation","menstrual","placenta","pregnancy"],
  "The male gamete is the sperm, produced in the testes; the female gamete is the ovum, released at ovulation. Fertilisation (union of sperm and ovum) occurs in the oviduct. The zygote implants in the uterus, where the placenta supplies nutrients and oxygen and removes waste. The menstrual cycle is governed by hormones and rarely lasts exactly 28 days for every woman.",
  [{"q":"Fertilisation in humans normally occurs in the…","o":["uterus","vagina","oviduct","ovary"],"a":2,"e":"The sperm meets the ovum in the oviduct (fallopian tube)."},{"q":"Which organ produces the ovum?","o":["uterus","ovary","oviduct","vagina"],"a":1,"e":"The ovaries produce ova and the hormones oestrogen and progesterone."}]],
 ["Genetics & Evolution", ["gene","allele","dominant","recessive","variation","natural selection"],
  "Genes on chromosomes carry hereditary information; alleles are alternative forms of a gene. In a monohybrid cross, the dominant allele masks the recessive one, giving the classic 3:1 ratio of offspring. Variation within a species comes from mutation and sexual reproduction; natural selection favours useful variations so that over time populations evolve.",
  [{"q":"In a monohybrid cross of two heterozygotes (Tt × Tt), the phenotypic ratio is…","o":["1:1","3:1","1:2:1","9:3:3:1"],"a":1,"e":"Three show the dominant trait, one shows the recessive: 3:1."},{"q":"The physical appearance of an organism is its…","o":["genotype","phenotype","gene","chromosome"],"a":1,"e":"Phenotype is the observable trait; genotype is the genetic makeup."}]]
],
"Chemistry": [
 ["Electrolysis & Electricity", ["electrolysis","electrolyte","anode","cathode","ions","current"],
  "Electrolysis breaks down an electrolyte (molten or dissolved ionic compound) using electricity. The cathode (negative electrode) attracts cations, which gain electrons; the anode (positive electrode) attracts anions, which lose electrons. For example, electrolysis of molten NaCl gives sodium at the cathode and chlorine at the anode. The amount of product depends on the quantity of charge passed.",
  [{"q":"During electrolysis, cations migrate to the…","o":["anode","cathode","both electrodes","neither electrode"],"a":1,"e":"Cations are positive, so they move to the negative electrode (cathode)."},
   {"q":"The gas given off at the anode during electrolysis of dilute H2SO4 is…","o":["hydrogen","oxygen","sulphur dioxide","water vapour"],"a":1,"e":"Hydroxide ions are discharged at the anode to give oxygen."}]],
 ["Metals & Their Extraction", ["metal","ore","smelting","blast furnace","reduction","reactivity"],
  "Metals are extracted from their ores; the method depends on reactivity. Unreactive metals such as gold occur native. Metals from zinc upwards are extracted by reduction with carbon or electrolysis: iron comes from haematite in the blast furnace and aluminium from bauxite by electrolysis. The reactivity series (K > Na > Ca > Mg > Al > C > Zn > Fe > H > Cu > Ag > Au) predicts displacement reactions.",
  [{"q":"Iron is extracted from haematite in the…","o":["open hearth","blast furnace","fractional column","flotation tank"],"a":1,"e":"The blast furnace reduces haematite with carbon monoxide."},
   {"q":"Which metal is extracted by electrolysis of its molten ore?","o":["iron","copper","aluminium","zinc"],"a":2,"e":"Aluminium is too reactive for carbon reduction, so electrolysis is used."}]],
 ["Organic Chemistry: Alkanes", ["alkane","hydrocarbon","saturated","methane","homologous","fractional distillation"],
  "Alkanes are saturated hydrocarbons of general formula CnH2n+2: methane CH4, ethane C2H6, propane C3H8. They are obtained from crude oil by fractional distillation and burn to give carbon dioxide and water. Their main reaction is substitution with halogens in sunlight; as members of a homologous series they differ by a CH2 unit.",
  [{"q":"Which of these is an alkane?","o":["C2H4","C2H6","C2H2","C6H6"],"a":1,"e":"C2H6 is ethane, an alkane with formula CnH2n+2 for n=2."},{"q":"Crude oil is separated into fractions by…","o":["filtration","fractional distillation","electrolysis","crystallisation"],"a":1,"e":"Fractional distillation separates crude oil by boiling point."}]],
 ["Rates of Reaction", ["rate","catalyst","concentration","temperature","surface area","collision"],
  "The rate of a reaction increases with higher concentration, higher temperature, smaller particle size (greater surface area) and the presence of a catalyst. Collision theory explains why: particles must collide with enough energy (activation energy) to react, and these factors increase the frequency or energy of effective collisions.",
  [{"q":"Which change increases the rate of a reaction most?","o":["lowering the temperature","adding a catalyst","diluting the reactants","using larger lumps"],"a":1,"e":"A catalyst lowers activation energy, speeding up the reaction without being used up."},{"q":"Powdered calcium carbonate reacts faster than lumps because it has more…","o":["mass","surface area","density","volume"],"a":1,"e":"Fine particles expose more surface area for collisions."}]],
 ["Reversible Reactions & Equilibrium", ["equilibrium","reversible","Le Chatelier","limestone","entropy"],
  "Many reactions are reversible and shown with ⇌. At equilibrium the forward and backward rates are equal, so concentrations stay constant. Le Chatelier's principle: a system at equilibrium shifts to oppose a change — increasing concentration or pressure shifts it to the side with fewer molecules/less of the added substance; increasing temperature favours the endothermic direction.",
  [{"q":"Increasing the pressure of N2 + 3H2 ⇌ 2NH3 shifts the equilibrium…","o":["to the left","to the right","not at all","randomly"],"a":1,"e":"The forward side has fewer gas molecules (4 → 2), so higher pressure favours it."},{"q":"At equilibrium, the rates of the forward and backward reactions are…","o":["zero","equal","unequal","increasing"],"a":1,"e":"Equilibrium is dynamic: both reactions continue at equal rates."}]],
 ["Water & Solutions", ["water","solution","solute","solvent","concentration","hard water"],
  "Water is a universal solvent because it is polar. A solution is a solute dissolved in a solvent; concentration is the amount of solute per volume of solution. Hard water contains dissolved calcium and magnesium salts, which stop soap lathering; it can be softened by boiling (temporary hardness) or ion exchange. Water boils at 100°C at 1 atm.",
  [{"q":"Hardness of water that is removed by boiling is called…","o":["permanent","temporary","artificial","mineral"],"a":1,"e":"Boiling removes temporary hardness caused by hydrogencarbonates."},{"q":"A solution is a mixture of a solute and a…","o":["solvent","precipitate","suspension","filtrate"],"a":0,"e":"The solute dissolves in the solvent to form the solution."}]]
],
"Physics": [
 ["Vectors & Scalars", ["vector","scalar","displacement","velocity","force","resultant"],
  "Quantities with only size are scalars (distance, speed, mass, time). Quantities with size and direction are vectors (displacement, velocity, acceleration, force). Vectors add by head-to-tail or by resolution: the resultant of two perpendicular vectors is found from R² = X² + Y² and tanθ = Y/X.",
  [{"q":"Which of these is a vector quantity?","o":["speed","distance","displacement","mass"],"a":2,"e":"Displacement has both magnitude and direction, so it is a vector."},
   {"q":"A force of 3 N east and 4 N north act together. The resultant is…","o":["5 N at 53° to the east","7 N","1 N","5 N due north"],"a":0,"e":"R = √(3²+4²) = 5 N, at tan⁻¹(4/3) ≈ 53° to the east."}]],
 ["Projectile Motion", ["projectile","trajectory","gravity","range","maximum height","parabola"],
  "A projectile is an object moving under gravity alone. Its horizontal motion is uniform (constant velocity) while vertical motion is uniform acceleration g = 9.8 m/s². The path is a parabola. Time of flight, maximum height and range follow from the initial velocity and angle of projection; at maximum height the vertical velocity is zero.",
  [{"q":"At the maximum height of a projectile, the vertical velocity is…","o":["maximum","zero","equal to horizontal velocity","negative"],"a":1,"e":"The projectile stops rising (v=0) at the top before falling."},{"q":"The horizontal motion of a projectile is…","o":["accelerating","uniform","decelerating","zero"],"a":1,"e":"No horizontal force acts, so horizontal velocity stays constant."}]],
 ["Energy, Work & Power", ["work","energy","power","joule","watt","conservation"],
  "Work is done when a force moves a body: W = F × d (joules). Energy is the capacity to do work, in kinetic (½mv²), potential (mgh) and other forms; the principle of conservation states energy is neither created nor destroyed, only converted. Power is the rate of doing work: P = W/t (watts), and efficiency = useful output ÷ total input × 100%.",
  [{"q":"A force of 20 N moves a box 5 m. The work done is…","o":["4 J","25 J","100 J","15 J"],"a":2,"e":"W = 20 × 5 = 100 J."},
   {"q":"A machine uses 1000 J in 5 s. Its power is…","o":["200 W","5000 W","2000 W","5 W"],"a":0,"e":"P = 1000 ÷ 5 = 200 W."}]],
 ["Sound Waves", ["sound","frequency","pitch","loudness","echo","vibration"],
  "Sound is a longitudinal wave produced by vibration and needs a medium (it cannot travel in a vacuum). Its speed is about 340 m/s in air, faster in water and solids. Frequency determines pitch; amplitude determines loudness. An echo is a reflected sound; echo sounding uses the relation distance = (speed × time)/2.",
  [{"q":"Sound cannot travel through a…","o":["liquid","solid","vacuum","gas"],"a":2,"e":"Sound needs a material medium to travel; there is none in a vacuum."},{"q":"The speed of sound is greatest in…","o":["air","water","steel","a vacuum"],"a":2,"e":"Sound travels fastest in solids because particles are closer."}]],
 ["Light: Lenses & Mirrors", ["convex","concave","focal length","image","refraction","magnification"],
  "Convex (converging) lenses and concave mirrors can form real images that can be captured on a screen; concave (diverging) lenses and convex mirrors form only virtual images. The lens/mirror equation 1/f = 1/u + 1/v links focal length f, object distance u and image distance v; magnification m = v/u = hi/ho.",
  [{"q":"Which lens always forms a virtual, upright and diminished image?","o":["convex","concave","converging","biconvex"],"a":1,"e":"A concave (diverging) lens always gives a virtual, upright, diminished image."},{"q":"The image formed by a plane mirror is…","o":["real and inverted","virtual and same size","magnified","blurred"],"a":1,"e":"A plane mirror forms a virtual, upright, same-size image."}]],
 ["Electronics & Semiconductors", ["semiconductor","diode","transistor","doping","p-n junction","rectifier"],
  "Semiconductors (silicon, germanium) conduct better than insulators but worse than metals. Doping adds impurities to make n-type (extra electrons) or p-type (holes) material. A p-n junction diode allows current one way only and is used as a rectifier to change AC to DC; a transistor amplifies or switches signals.",
  [{"q":"A diode is used in a circuit mainly to…","o":["amplify signals","rectify alternating current","store charge","resist current"],"a":1,"e":"A diode conducts in one direction only, so it converts AC to DC."},{"q":"Adding impurities to a pure semiconductor is called…","o":["doping","fusion","refining","ionising"],"a":0,"e":"Doping adds impurities to control conductivity."}]]
]
,
"Agricultural Science": [
 ["Farm Tools & Machinery", ["tool","machinery","tractor","implement","cultivation","maintenance"],
  "Farm tools are hand implements such as cutlasses, hoes, rakes and watering cans, used for small-scale work. Machinery includes tractors and implements (plough, harrow, ridger) powered by engines and used for large-scale cultivation. Machinery increases output and speed but needs capital, fuel, skilled operators and regular maintenance.",
  [{"q":"Which of these is a farm machine?","o":["hoe","cutlass","tractor","rake"],"a":2,"e":"A tractor is powered machinery; the others are hand tools."},
   {"q":"The main advantage of farm machinery is…","o":["cheap initial cost","increased speed of work","no maintenance needed","works on any soil"],"a":1,"e":"Machinery covers land faster and more uniformly, raising output per labour-hour."}]],
 ["Pests & Diseases of Crops", ["pest","disease","control","insecticide","fungicide","infestation"],
  "Crop pests include insects (stem borers, grasshoppers), rodents, birds and nematodes; diseases are caused by fungi, bacteria and viruses. Damage appears as holes, wilting, rots and stunted growth. Control methods are cultural (crop rotation, clean seed), biological (natural enemies), chemical (insecticides and fungicides) and mechanical (traps, physical removal).",
  [{"q":"A chemical used to control fungal crop diseases is called a…","o":["insecticide","herbicide","fungicide","rodenticide"],"a":2,"e":"Fungicides target fungi; insecticides target insects."},{"q":"Growing different crops in alternate seasons to break pest life cycles is…","o":["monoculture","crop rotation","bush burning","mulching"],"a":1,"e":"Crop rotation is a cultural control method."}]],
 ["Animal Nutrition & Feeds", ["feed","ration","nutrients","concentrate","roughage","supplement"],
  "Farm animals need carbohydrates, proteins, fats, minerals, vitamins and water. Feeds fall into roughages (grass, hay, silage — bulky, fibrous) and concentrates (grains, oilseed cakes — rich in energy or protein). A ration is the daily feed allowance; a balanced ration supplies all nutrients in the right proportions for production or growth.",
  [{"q":"Which of these is a concentrate feed?","o":["fresh grass","maize grain","silage","straw"],"a":1,"e":"Maize grain is an energy-rich concentrate; grass and silage are roughages."},{"q":"A feed rich in protein for growing animals is…","o":["groundnut cake","straw","silage","sawdust"],"a":0,"e":"Groundnut cake is a protein-rich concentrate."}]],
 ["Poultry & Livestock Production", ["poultry","broiler","layer","housing","vaccination","management"],
  "Poultry includes broilers (for meat) and layers (for eggs); deep litter and battery cage systems are both used in Nigeria. Good management requires proper housing, clean water, balanced feed, lighting and routine vaccination against Newcastle disease and fowl pox. Cattle and goats are kept for meat, milk and hides under extensive or intensive systems.",
  [{"q":"A layer is a bird kept for…","o":["meat","eggs","both eggs and meat","breeding only"],"a":1,"e":"Layers are managed for egg production; broilers for meat."},{"q":"Newcastle disease in poultry is prevented by…","o":["vaccination","dipping","deworming","drenching"],"a":0,"e":"Routine vaccination protects against Newcastle disease."}]],
 ["Fisheries & Fish Farming", ["fish","pond","aquaculture","fingerling","feeding","harvest"],
  "Fisheries include capture from rivers and the sea and aquaculture (fish farming). Ponds are stocked with fingerlings, fed with formulated feed, and must have good water quality (oxygen, pH, temperature). Common farmed species in Nigeria are tilapia and catfish. Harvesting is by netting or draining, and fish spoil quickly without preservation.",
  [{"q":"The young fish stocked into a pond are called…","o":["fingerlings","fry traps","cob","juveniles only"],"a":0,"e":"Fingerlings are the young fish of stocking size."},{"q":"Which water condition is most important for fish survival?","o":["colour","dissolved oxygen","turbidity only","temperature constant at 30°C"],"a":1,"e":"Adequate dissolved oxygen is essential; fish die without it."}]],
 ["Farm Management & Records", ["management","record","labour","budget","planning","enterprise"],
  "Good farm management plans the use of land, labour, capital and equipment to maximise profit. Records are kept for production, sales, labour and finances, enabling the farmer to compare enterprises and make decisions. Records include farm diary, inventory, labour and cash books, and they support credit applications and taxes.",
  [{"q":"Which farm record shows the value of equipment, animals and produce?","o":["labour record","inventory","cash book","diary"],"a":1,"e":"An inventory lists assets and their values at a point in time."},{"q":"A statement of income and expenses over a period is a…","o":["cash book","inventory","balance sheet only","farm plan"],"a":0,"e":"The cash book records day-to-day money received and paid."}]
]],
"Economics": [
 ["Elasticity of Demand & Supply", ["elasticity","price","demand","supply","responsive","revenue"],
  "Price elasticity of demand measures how much quantity demanded responds to a price change: PED = %ΔQ ÷ %ΔP. Demand is elastic (PED > 1) when consumers switch easily, inelastic (PED < 1) for necessities. For a producer: if demand is elastic, a price cut raises total revenue; if inelastic, a price rise raises revenue. Elasticity of supply depends on time and production flexibility.",
  [{"q":"If a 10% price cut raises quantity demanded by 25%, demand is…","o":["inelastic","elastic","unitary","perfectly inelastic"],"a":1,"e":"PED = 25÷10 = 2.5 > 1, so demand is elastic."},
   {"q":"Taxing a good with inelastic demand shifts the burden mostly to…","o":["the producer","the consumer","the government","exporters"],"a":1,"e":"Consumers keep buying despite the price rise, so they bear most of the tax."}]],
 ["Inflation & Unemployment", ["inflation","unemployment","price level","CPI","full employment","deflation"],
  "Inflation is a persistent rise in the general price level, measured by the Consumer Price Index; it erodes the purchasing power of money. Demand-pull inflation follows excess demand, cost-push follows rising costs. Unemployment is the number of able-bodied people willing and able to work who cannot find jobs; types include frictional, structural and cyclical. High inflation and unemployment together are stagflation.",
  [{"q":"A persistent rise in the general price level is called…","o":["deflation","inflation","stagflation","recession"],"a":1,"e":"Inflation is the sustained upward movement of the general price level."},
   {"q":"Unemployment caused by changes in the structure of industry is…","o":["frictional","structural","cyclical","seasonal"],"a":1,"e":"Structural unemployment follows the decline of old industries and the rise of new skills."}]],
 ["Public Finance & Taxation", ["tax","fiscal","budget","direct","indirect","public debt"],
  "Public finance studies government revenue and expenditure. Revenue comes from taxes (direct — paid by the taxpayer, like income tax; indirect — levied on goods, like VAT), fees, fines and borrowing. A budget is the planned revenue and expenditure for a year; a deficit budget spends more than it earns. Progressive taxes take a larger share of higher incomes and reduce inequality.",
  [{"q":"Which of these is a direct tax?","o":["VAT","import duty","income tax","excise duty"],"a":2,"e":"Income tax is paid directly by the taxpayer to the government."},
   {"q":"A budget in which planned expenditure exceeds revenue is…","o":["surplus","balanced","deficit","supplementary"],"a":2,"e":"Expenditure greater than revenue is a deficit budget."}]],
 ["International Trade", ["export","import","balance of payment","terms of trade","comparative advantage","quota"],
  "International trade is the exchange of goods and services between countries, based on comparative advantage — each country produces goods it can make relatively cheaply. Exports earn foreign exchange; imports use it. The balance of trade is exports minus imports of goods; the balance of payments adds services, transfers and capital flows. Restrictions include tariffs, quotas and embargoes.",
  [{"q":"The value of exports less the value of imports of goods is the…","o":["balance of payments","balance of trade","terms of trade","current account"],"a":1,"e":"The balance of trade covers visible (goods) trade only."},
   {"q":"A government limit on the quantity of an import is a…","o":["tariff","quota","dumping","devaluation"],"a":1,"e":"A quota restricts quantity; a tariff is a tax on imports."}]],
 ["Money & Banking", ["money","currency","bank","credit","interest","liquidity"],
  "Money performs four functions: medium of exchange, measure of value, store of value and standard of deferred payment. Its qualities are acceptability, portability, durability, divisibility and relative scarcity. Commercial banks accept deposits, give loans and create credit; the central bank issues currency, controls credit, holds reserves and acts as banker to the government.",
  [{"q":"Which function of money allows comparing the worth of two goods?","o":["medium of exchange","measure of value","store of value","deferred payment"],"a":1,"e":"Money acts as a common yardstick for valuing goods."},
   {"q":"The bank that issues currency in Nigeria is the…","o":["First Bank of Nigeria","Central Bank of Nigeria","Commercial banks","Bank of Industry"],"a":1,"e":"Only the central bank issues legal tender."}]],
 ["National Income", ["GDP","GNP","gross domestic","per capita","output","net factor"],
  "National income is the total value of goods and services produced in a country in a year. GDP measures production within the country's borders; GNP adds net income earned abroad. Per capita income is national income ÷ population, and is used to compare living standards, though it hides inequality. Measurement uses the output, income and expenditure approaches, which should agree.",
  [{"q":"The total value of goods and services produced within a country's borders in a year is…","o":["GNP","GDP","NNP","per capita income"],"a":1,"e":"GDP is domestic production; GNP adds net factor income from abroad."},
   {"q":"Per capita income is obtained by dividing national income by…","o":["total imports","population","total savings","number of workers"],"a":1,"e":"Per capita income = national income ÷ population."}]
]],
"Government": [
 ["The Executive", ["executive","president","implementation","cabinet","prerogative","appointment"],
  "The executive implements the laws and runs the government. In a presidential system, a directly elected president is both head of state and head of government and appoints ministers; in a parliamentary system, the prime minister is drawn from the legislature and is answerable to it. The executive's powers include appointment, pardon, foreign policy and command of the armed forces.",
  [{"q":"The organ of government that implements laws is the…","o":["legislature","judiciary","executive","civil service only"],"a":2,"e":"The executive implements and administers the laws made by the legislature."},{"q":"In a presidential system the head of government is the…","o":["prime minister","president","speaker","chief justice"],"a":1,"e":"The president is both head of state and head of government."}]],
 ["The Legislature", ["legislature","parliament","bicameral","unicameral","bill","oversight"],
  "The legislature makes laws, approves budgets and checks the executive. Nigeria's National Assembly is bicameral: the Senate (108 members) and the House of Representatives (360 members). A bill is debated, passed by both chambers and assented to by the president before becoming law. Other functions include ratification of treaties and oversight of the executive via committees.",
  [{"q":"Nigeria's National Assembly is called bicameral because it has…","o":["one chamber","two chambers","three chambers","four chambers"],"a":1,"e":"The Senate and House of Representatives form two chambers."},{"q":"A proposed law before parliament is a…","o":["decree","bill","treaty","writ"],"a":1,"e":"A bill becomes an act of parliament after passage and assent."}]],
 ["The Judiciary", ["judiciary","court","judge","independence","appeal","review"],
  "The judiciary interprets the law, settles disputes and reviews the actions of the other arms. Its independence is protected by security of tenure, fixed salaries and the separation of powers. In Nigeria, the Supreme Court is the highest court, followed by the Court of Appeal, Federal High Courts and State High Courts. Judicial review declares acts of the executive or legislature unconstitutional.",
  [{"q":"The highest court in Nigeria is the…","o":["Court of Appeal","Federal High Court","Supreme Court","Sharia Court of Appeal"],"a":2,"e":"The Supreme Court is the final appellate court."},{"q":"Which court first hears most criminal cases in Nigeria?","o":["Supreme Court","Magistrate Court","Court of Appeal","Federal High Court of Appeal"],"a":1,"e":"Magistrate courts try most minor criminal cases at first instance."}]],
 ["Federalism", ["federalism","states","delegated","residual","concurrent","secession"],
  "Federalism divides power between a central government and component units (states). The constitution lists exclusive powers (defence, currency, foreign affairs), concurrent powers (education, health) for both levels, and residual powers left to the states. Federalism suits a large, heterogeneous country like Nigeria, but it can breed duplication and friction.",
  [{"q":"Which of these is an exclusive power in a federation?","o":["education","defence","health","agriculture"],"a":1,"e":"Defence, currency and foreign affairs are exclusive to the centre."},{"q":"In a federation, residual powers are exercised by the…","o":["central government","component units","judiciary","traditional rulers"],"a":1,"e":"Residual matters not listed in the constitution belong to the states."}]],
 ["The Constitution & Rule of Law", ["constitution","rule of law","entrenched","supreme","rights","legality"],
  "A constitution is the body of fundamental rules by which a state is governed; in a written constitution, it is supreme and entrenched (hard to amend). Rule of law means the supremacy of law over every person, equality before the law and the fair enforcement of fundamental human rights. Its limits include emergency powers and sovereign immunity.",
  [{"q":"A constitutional provision that cannot be easily amended is said to be…","o":["flexible","entrenched","unwritten","customary"],"a":1,"e":"Entrenched clauses need special majorities to change."},{"q":"Which of these promotes the rule of law?","o":["immunity for officials","independent courts","secret trials","arbitrary arrest"],"a":1,"e":"An independent judiciary and equality before the law uphold it."}]],
 ["Local Government", ["local government","council","chairman","ward","rates","grassroots"],
  "Local government is the third tier of government, closest to the people. A local government council, headed by a chairman with councillors from wards, provides primary education support, health centres, roads, markets, sanitation and revenue collection (rates, licences). Section 7 of the 1999 Constitution guarantees democratically elected local governments; they are funded by statutory allocations, internally generated revenue and grants.",
  [{"q":"The head of a local government council is the…","o":["governor","chairman","president","senator"],"a":1,"e":"A democratically elected chairman heads the council."},{"q":"Local governments are funded mainly by…","o":["exports","statutory allocations","licence fees only","foreign aid"],"a":1,"e":"Revenue comes from statutory allocations and internally generated revenue."}]
]],
"Literature in English": [
 ["Non-African Prose Study", ["novel","setting","character","theme","non-African","author"],
  "Non-African prose studied in Nigerian schools includes works by non-African novelists. Analysis follows the elements of fiction: plot, setting, characterisation, theme, point of view, language and style. Candidates should locate the background of the work, describe the major characters with evidence, discuss two or more themes and relate the work to human experience.",
  [{"q":"In studying a novel, the underlying message of the work is its…","o":["plot","theme","setting","style"],"a":1,"e":"The theme is the central idea or message the writer explores."},{"q":"The vantage point from which a story is told is its…","o":["plot","point of view","setting","conflict"],"a":1,"e":"Point of view is the narrative perspective (first or third person)."}]],
 ["African Poetry Study", ["poem","African","imagery","tone","rhythm","oral tradition"],
  "African poetry draws on oral tradition — proverbs, songs, praise poetry and rhythm. Analysis covers speaker, tone, theme, imagery, diction and sound devices. Praise poetry celebrates heroes; protest poetry challenges oppression. When answering, quote briefly to support every claim about effect or meaning.",
  [{"q":"Praise poetry in African tradition primarily…","o":["mocks a ruler","celebrates heroes and achievements","teaches grammar","narrates wars only"],"a":1,"e":"Praise poetry honours persons, clans or achievements, often with hyperbole."},{"q":"Repetition of initial consonant sounds in a poem is called…","o":["assonance","alliteration","rhyme","metre"],"a":1,"e":"Alliteration repeats initial consonant sounds."}]],
 ["Unseen Poetry Analysis", ["unseen","comprehension","figure of speech","mood","appreciation","paraphrase"],
  "Unseen poetry tests appreciation without prior study. Steps: read twice, identify the speaker and situation, note the dominant mood, paraphrase the poem, then discuss the figures of speech and sound devices and their effect. Always answer in your own words and support points with short quotations.",
  [{"q":"The first thing to identify in an unseen poem is…","o":["the rhyme scheme only","the speaker and subject","the date of publication","the poet's biography"],"a":1,"e":"Understanding what the poem is about and who speaks comes before technique."},{"q":"The overall feeling created by a poem is its…","o":["subject","mood","rhyme","diction"],"a":1,"e":"Mood is the emotional atmosphere of the poem."}]],
 ["Literary Devices & Figures", ["metaphor","simile","irony","symbolism","allusion","imagery"],
  "Literary devices shape meaning: simile and metaphor compare; irony says one thing and means another; symbolism uses objects for abstract ideas; allusion refers to a known person or event; imagery appeals to the senses; and onomatopoeia imitates sounds. Naming the device is only half the answer — the examiner wants its effect in the passage.",
  [{"q":"My love is a red, red rose is an example of…","o":["simile","metaphor","allusion","hyperbole"],"a":1,"e":"The direct comparison without like or as makes it a metaphor."},{"q":"Words that imitate sounds, like buzz, are…","o":["onomatopoeia","simile","hyperbole","paradox"],"a":0,"e":"Onomatopoeia imitates natural sounds."}]],
 ["Comparative Analysis", ["compare","contrast","similar","different","structure","both texts"],
  "Comparative questions demand parallel treatment: point by point, a similarity or difference, with evidence from both texts. Compare themes, characters, tone, techniques and structure; avoid treating the texts one after the other without linking. A conclusion should state the overall relationship between the works.",
  [{"q":"When answering a comparative question, the best structure is…","o":["a full summary of one text","point-by-point comparison with evidence","a list of titles","only personal opinion"],"a":1,"e":"Parallel points with evidence from both texts earn the marks."},{"q":"A question asking how two texts are alike and different is…","o":["descriptive","comparative","narrative","rhetorical"],"a":1,"e":"Comparative questions test similarities and differences."}]],
 ["Textual Evidence & Style", ["quotation","evidence","style","diction","prose","analysis"],
  "Marks in literature depend on evidence. Quote short phrases or line numbers and link each to the point being made. Style covers the writer's diction (word choice), sentence patterns, imagery and narrative techniques. A well-integrated quotation — not long copying — shows close reading and wins marks.",
  [{"q":"A short quotation is used in literary answers mainly to…","o":["fill the page","support the point being made","show memorisation","avoid thinking"],"a":1,"e":"Evidence from the text is the basis of every valid literary claim."},{"q":"The writer's choice of words is his or her…","o":["diction","plot","setting","dialogue"],"a":0,"e":"Diction is word choice; it shapes tone and style."}]
]],
"Geography": [
 ["Map Reading & Interpretation", ["map","scale","contour","bearing","grid","relief"],
  "Map reading uses scale (ratio, statement or linear), grid reference, bearings and symbols. Contour lines show relief: close contours mean steep slopes, wide contours gentle slopes; their pattern identifies hills, valleys, spurs and passes. Local relief and drainage are read from contour form, and distances are measured along the route using the scale.",
  [{"q":"Contour lines that are very close together indicate…","o":["gentle slope","steep slope","flat land","a river"],"a":1,"e":"The closer the contours, the steeper the gradient."},
   {"q":"The vertical interval on a map is the…","o":["height of the highest point","difference between two adjacent contours","length of the map","number of grid squares"],"a":1,"e":"The vertical interval is the constant difference in height between successive contours."}]],
 ["Weather & Climate", ["weather","climate","rainfall","temperature","instruments","forecast"],
  "Weather is the day-to-day condition of the atmosphere; climate is the average weather over many years. Elements measured are temperature, rainfall, pressure, wind, humidity and sunshine, using the thermometer, rain gauge, barometer, anemometer, hygrometer and sunshine recorder. Weather instruments are kept in a Stevenson screen to give standard readings.",
  [{"q":"Rainfall is measured with a…","o":["barometer","rain gauge","anemometer","hygrometer"],"a":1,"e":"The rain gauge collects rain; the anemometer measures wind speed."},{"q":"The average weather condition of a place over many years is its…","o":["weather","climate","season","forecast"],"a":1,"e":"Climate is the long-term average; weather is day-to-day."}]],
 ["Population & Settlement", ["population","birth rate","density","migration","settlement","growth"],
  "Population study covers size, distribution, density and growth (births, deaths, migration). Nigeria's population is large and youthful, with rapid growth. Overpopulation, underpopulation and optimum population each compare population to resources. Settlements grow from villages to cities where functions (trade, administration, industry) concentrate; urbanisation brings pressure on housing and services.",
  [{"q":"Population density is…","o":["total births in a year","people per unit area","urban population only","migrants in a year"],"a":1,"e":"Density = population ÷ area, usually people per km²."},{"q":"People moving out of a country to live in another is…","o":["immigration","emigration","transhumance","commuting"],"a":1,"e":"Emigration is leaving; immigration is entering."}]],
 ["River Landforms & Delta", ["river","erosion","deposition","delta","meander","floodplain"],
  "Rivers erode, transport and deposit. Landforms include valleys, gorges, waterfalls, meanders, ox-bow lakes, levees and floodplains. A delta forms where a river deposits sediment on entering a slow or still water body (sea or lake), building up distributaries and swampy islands, as in the Niger Delta. Deltas are fertile but prone to flooding.",
  [{"q":"A delta is formed mainly by…","o":["erosion","deposition","volcanism","tectonic uplift"],"a":1,"e":"Deposition of sediment at a river mouth builds a delta."},{"q":"A bend in a river is called a…","o":["levee","meander","gorge","delta"],"a":1,"e":"Meanders are bends that migrate across the floodplain."}]],
 ["Industrial & Economic Geography", ["industry","location","raw material","market","labour","agglomeration"],
  "Location factors for industry are raw materials, power, market, labour, transport and government policy. Primary industries extract (mining, farming); secondary industries manufacture; tertiary industries provide services. In Nigeria, industry is concentrated around Lagos, Port Harcourt, Kano and Kaduna, and faces problems of power, infrastructure and imported inputs.",
  [{"q":"Cement factories in Nigeria are located near limestone because of…","o":["market","raw materials","labour","capital"],"a":1,"e":"Limestone is the main raw material, so plants locate near deposits."},{"q":"Which industry processes raw materials into finished goods?","o":["primary","secondary","tertiary","quaternary only"],"a":1,"e":"Secondary (manufacturing) industry transforms raw materials."}]],
 ["Environmental Hazards & Conservation", ["hazard","erosion","desertification","flood","pollution","conservation"],
  "Environmental hazards include soil erosion, desertification, flooding, drought and pollution. Desertification advances where vegetation is cleared, overgrazing occurs and the climate dries. Conservation practices are afforestation, controlled grazing, terracing, waste recycling and pollution control, which protect resources for future generations.",
  [{"q":"The major cause of desertification in the Sahel is…","o":["industrialisation","overgrazing and deforestation","tourism","ice melting"],"a":1,"e":"Removing vegetation cover exposes soil to wind and drought."},{"q":"Afforestation is the practice of…","o":["cutting trees","planting trees","burning bushes","flooding land"],"a":1,"e":"Tree planting checks soil erosion and desertification."}]
]],
"Commerce": [
 ["Insurance", ["insurance","premium","policy","risk","underwriting","indemnity"],
  "Insurance protects against risk: many pay small premiums so the few who suffer loss are compensated. Key principles are insurable interest, utmost good faith, indemnity and subrogation. Types include fire, marine, life, motor, accident and burglary insurance. The insurer issues a policy; a claim must be supported by proof of loss.",
  [{"q":"The sum a policyholder pays for insurance cover is the…","o":["claim","premium","policy","commission"],"a":1,"e":"The premium is the fee paid for the insurance policy."},
   {"q":"Which principle requires the insured to have a real interest in the subject of the insurance?","o":["indemnity","utmost good faith","insurable interest","subrogation"],"a":2,"e":"You can only insure something whose loss would harm you."}]],
 ["Stock Exchange & Securities", ["stock","shares","securities","broker","dividend","capital market"],
  "The stock exchange is the market where shares, stocks and bonds are bought and sold, forming the capital market. Shares raise capital for companies; holders receive dividends. A broker deals on behalf of clients. Trading is regulated by the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) in Nigeria. Prices move with demand and supply.",
  [{"q":"The reward of a shareholder in a company is a…","o":["salary","dividend","interest","commission"],"a":1,"e":"Shareholders earn dividends from profits."},{"q":"The market where existing shares are bought and sold is the…","o":["money market","stock exchange","commodity market","labour market"],"a":1,"e":"The stock exchange is the secondary market for securities."}]],
 ["Transport & Communication", ["transport","communication","warehouse","freight","logistics","postal"],
  "Transport moves goods and people by road, rail, water, air and pipeline; communication carries information via post, telephone, internet and broadcasting. Efficient transport and communication lower costs, widen markets and speed business decisions. Warehouses store goods before distribution and are sited near markets or ports.",
  [{"q":"The cheapest means of transporting heavy bulky goods over long distances is usually…","o":["air","road","water","pipeline"],"a":2,"e":"Water transport carries heavy cargo cheapest, though it is slow."},{"q":"Which is a means of communication?","o":["railway","telephone","pipeline","aircraft"],"a":1,"e":"Telephone carries information, not goods."}]],
 ["International Trade & Finance", ["export","import","document","letter of credit","bill of lading","exchange"],
  "International trade brings in goods a country cannot produce cheaply and earns foreign exchange from exports. Documents include the proforma invoice, bill of lading, bill of exchange, certificate of origin and letter of credit, which assures exporters of payment. Balance of payments records all transactions with the rest of the world.",
  [{"q":"Which document is a written order of payment by the importer's bank?","o":["bill of lading","letter of credit","certificate of origin","proforma invoice"],"a":1,"e":"A letter of credit guarantees payment to the exporter through banks."},{"q":"A tax on imported goods is called a…","o":["excise duty","import duty","income tax","capital tax"],"a":1,"e":"Import (customs) duties are levied on goods entering a country."}]],
 ["Business Units", ["sole proprietorship","partnership","limited liability","company","cooperative","public corporation"],
  "Business units are sole proprietorship (one owner, unlimited liability), partnership (2–20 owners sharing profit and liability), registered companies (limited liability, separate legal personality), cooperatives (member-owned) and public corporations (government-owned). Each differs in capital, control, continuity and liability, which is exactly what examiners test.",
  [{"q":"Which business unit has limited liability and a separate legal personality?","o":["sole proprietorship","partnership","registered company","informal trader"],"a":2,"e":"A registered company is a legal person distinct from its owners."},{"q":"The maximum number of partners in an ordinary partnership in Nigeria is…","o":["2","5","20","50"],"a":2,"e":"An ordinary partnership has 2–20 partners."}]],
 ["Business Finance & Accounting", ["capital","loan","overdraft","assets","liabilities","balance sheet"],
  "Business finance sources are personal savings, retained profit, loans, overdrafts, trade credit, hire purchase and shares. A balance sheet shows assets (what the business owns) and liabilities (what it owes); assets minus liabilities equals capital. Working capital is current assets minus current liabilities — the funds for day-to-day running.",
  [{"q":"Working capital equals…","o":["total assets","current assets minus current liabilities","fixed assets","capital plus loan"],"a":1,"e":"Working capital funds daily operations: current assets less current liabilities."},{"q":"Money owed by a business to others are its…","o":["assets","liabilities","capital","reserves"],"a":1,"e":"Liabilities are debts: loans, creditors and overdrafts."}]
]],
"Computer Studies": [
 ["Word Processing", ["word","document","format","font","spelling","print"],
  "Word processors (Microsoft Word, Google Docs) create, edit, format and print text documents. Features include fonts and sizes, alignment, bullet lists, tables, images, headers and footers, spell check, mail merge and print preview. Use Save As to keep older versions, and follow the file extension (.docx) conventions. Two Ss in processor: it processes words.",
  [{"q":"Which of these is word-processing software?","o":["Microsoft Excel","Microsoft Word","PowerPoint","Photoshop"],"a":1,"e":"Word is the classic word processor; Excel is a spreadsheet."},{"q":"A standard word-processed letter file saves with the extension…","o":[".xls",".docx",".ppt",".png"],"a":1,"e":".docx is the Word format."}]],
 ["Spreadsheets", ["spreadsheet","cell","formula","function","chart","SUM"],
  "A spreadsheet is a grid of rows and columns; each cell holds a value or formula. Formulas begin with = and use cell references (=A2+B2); functions include SUM, AVERAGE, MAX, MIN and IF. Charts (bar, line, pie) visualise data, and absolute references ($A$1) keep a cell fixed when copying formulas.",
  [{"q":"A formula in a spreadsheet always begins with…","o":["a number","the = sign","a space","a letter"],"a":1,"e":"Formulas start with =, e.g. =SUM(A1:A5)."},
   {"q":"To add the values in cells A1 to A5, you use…","o":["=ADD(A1:A5)","=SUM(A1:A5)","=TOTAL(A1;A5)","A1:A5+"],"a":1,"e":"SUM is the function for adding a range of cells."}]],
 ["Database Concepts", ["database","table","record","field","query","primary key"],
  "A database is an organised collection of related data. Tables store records (rows) and fields (columns); a primary key uniquely identifies each record; a query selects and sorts records against conditions (WHERE). A Database Management System (DBMS) like Microsoft Access lets users create tables, forms and reports, and prevents redundancy and inconsistency.",
  [{"q":"A single row of data in a database table is called a…","o":["field","record","query","form"],"a":1,"e":"Each row is a record; each column is a field."},{"q":"A software that manages a database is a…","o":["CMS","DBMS","CSS","DNS"],"a":1,"e":"A Database Management System creates, stores and queries data."}]],
 ["The Internet & Networks", ["internet","network","browser","URL","server","protocol"],
  "The Internet is a global network of networks; the World Wide Web is the interlinked document system on it, viewed in a browser. A URL addresses a resource; HTTP/HTTPS carry web pages; email, FTP and VoIP are other Internet services. Networks share resources using LAN (local area) and WAN (wide area) topologies — star, bus and ring.",
  [{"q":"The software used to view web pages is a…","o":["browser","server","protocol","router"],"a":0,"e":"Browsers (Chrome, Edge, Firefox) render pages from the Web."},
   {"q":"A network confined to one building is a…","o":["WAN","LAN","MAN","PAN only"],"a":1,"e":"LAN = Local Area Network, e.g. a school computer lab."}]],
 ["Programming Basics", ["programming","algorithm","flowchart","variable","loop","pseudo-code"],
  "Programming gives a computer instructions. An algorithm is a step-by-step solution; flowcharts use symbols (oval start/stop, rectangle process, diamond decision). Variables store values; loops repeat statements; conditions (IF) choose paths; and programs are written in languages such as Python and BASIC. Bugs are syntax or logic errors found by testing.",
  [{"q":"In a flowchart, a decision is represented by a…","o":["rectangle","diamond","oval","arrow"],"a":1,"e":"Decision diamonds branch to yes/no paths."},
   {"q":"Which of these is a programming language?","o":["HTML only","Python","HTTP","DOCX"],"a":1,"e":"Python is a high-level programming language."}]],
 ["Data Security & Ethics", ["security","password","virus","backup","encryption","ethics"],
  "Data security protects information from loss and theft: strong passwords, access rights, encryption and regular backups. Computer viruses and malware spread through files and downloads; antivirus software and updates defend systems. Ethics covers honest use of computers — respecting copyright, privacy and netiquette — and computer misuse laws punish hacking and fraud.",
  [{"q":"The best defence against losing important files is…","o":["deleting old files","regular backups","sharing passwords","disabling updates"],"a":1,"e":"Backups restore data after loss, damage or attack."},{"q":"Making data unreadable to unauthorised users is called…","o":["compression","encryption","formatting","defragmentation"],"a":1,"e":"Encryption scrambles data; only the key reads it."}]
]],
"Civic Education": [
 ["Citizenship & National Identity", ["citizen","rights","duty","nationalism","identity","patriotism"],
  "A citizen is a legally recognised member of a state — by birth, registration or naturalisation — with rights (life, freedom, education) and duties (obeying laws, paying taxes, defending the nation). National identity is built on shared symbols, history and values; patriotism means loyalty and service to the country, shown by lawful conduct and participation.",
  [{"q":"Citizenship acquired by a foreigner who qualifies after living in a country is by…","o":["birth","registration","naturalisation","inheritance"],"a":2,"e":"Naturalisation grants citizenship to qualified foreigners."},{"q":"A person who belongs to a state with full rights and duties is a…","o":["visitor","citizen","resident","alien"],"a":1,"e":"Citizenship carries rights and duties in a state."}]],
 ["Rights, Duties & Obligations", ["rights","freedom","duty","responsibility","law","education"],
  "Fundamental human rights include the right to life, dignity, personal liberty, fair hearing, privacy and freedom of expression, movement and association. Duties and obligations are obeying laws, paying taxes, protecting public property, voting and defending the country. Rights and duties are two sides of citizenship: my rights impose duties on others and vice versa.",
  [{"q":"Which of these is a civic obligation?","o":["freedom of expression","paying taxes","right to life","freedom of movement"],"a":1,"e":"Paying taxes is a duty of citizens; the others are rights."},{"q":"Which of these is a fundamental human right?","o":["tax payment","right to life","jury service","voting duty"],"a":1,"e":"The right to life is inalienable and protects all persons."}]],
 ["Drug Abuse & Substance Use", ["drug","abuse","substance","addiction","rehabilitation","consequence"],
  "Drug abuse is the harmful use of drugs without medical need: cocaine, heroin, tramadol, cannabis and even misuse of prescribed medicines. It harms health (organ damage, addiction, mental illness), family and society, and fuels crime, cultism and road accidents. Prevention includes education, counselling, hobbies and positive peer groups; treatment includes rehabilitation and support services.",
  [{"q":"Which of these is a social consequence of drug abuse?","o":["better concentration","involvement in crime","stronger health","higher income"],"a":1,"e":"Drug abuse links to crime, cultism and broken relationships."},{"q":"One healthy way to resist peer pressure to use drugs is…","o":["isolation from everyone","joining positive clubs and hobbies","skipping school","copying friends"],"a":1,"e":"Positive activities and good friends protect against drug abuse."}]],
 ["Human Trafficking", ["trafficking","exploitation","forced labour","smuggling","victim","prosecution"],
  "Human trafficking is the recruitment, transport or harbouring of persons by force, deception or abuse of power for exploitation — forced labour, prostitution, begging or organ removal. It violates human dignity and is a crime under Nigerian law. Prevention requires awareness, education, strong enforcement and support for victims; report suspicious cases to NAPTIP.",
  [{"q":"The agency in Nigeria that fights human trafficking is…","o":["EFCC","NAPTIP","ICPC","NCDC"],"a":1,"e":"NAPTIP handles trafficking in persons."},{"q":"Human trafficking involves the victim in…","o":["voluntary work","exploitation","self-employment","education"],"a":1,"e":"Victims are exploited: forced labour, prostitution or begging."}]],
 ["Anti-Corruption Agencies", ["corruption","EFCC","ICPC","bribery","accountability","whistle"],
  "Corruption is the abuse of public office for private gain — bribery, embezzlement, fraud and nepotism. Nigeria's anti-corruption agencies include the EFCC (economic and financial crimes), the ICPC (public service corruption) and the Code of Conduct Bureau. Citizens fight corruption by refusing and reporting bribes, demanding accountability and practising integrity.",
  [{"q":"The EFCC investigates primarily…","o":["traffic offences","economic and financial crimes","examination fraud only","tax evasion only"],"a":1,"e":"EFCC tackles economic and financial crimes, including fraud and money laundering."},{"q":"Bribery is best described as…","o":["a gift freely given","giving money to influence official decisions","charity","tax"],"a":1,"e":"Bribery tries to buy favourable decisions."}]],
 ["Responsible Citizenship & Leadership", ["leadership","integrity","followership","service","example","mentor"],
  "Leadership is the ability to influence others toward good goals; good leaders show integrity, accountability, empathy and self-control. Followership is also civic: supporting lawful leadership, giving constructive feedback and refusing to follow bad orders. Society improves when citizens lead by example at home, school and work — mentoring juniors and serving communities.",
  [{"q":"The most important quality of a good leader is…","o":["wealth","integrity","popularity","strength"],"a":1,"e":"Integrity — honesty and consistency — underpins trust and good leadership."},{"q":"A citizen who reports corruption is practising…","o":["whistle-blowing","conspiracy","sedition","bias"],"a":0,"e":"Whistle-blowing exposes wrongdoing for the public good."}]
]
],
"Further Mathematics": [
 ["Polynomials & Remainder Theorem", ["polynomial","remainder","factor theorem","division","root","synthetic"],
  "A polynomial is an expression of the form aₙxⁿ + … + a₁x + a₀. The remainder theorem: when f(x) is divided by (x − a), the remainder is f(a). The factor theorem: (x − a) is a factor of f(x) if and only if f(a) = 0. These theorems factorise cubics and higher polynomials and solve equations without long division.",
  [{"q":"When f(x) = x³ − 2x + 1 is divided by (x − 1), the remainder is…","o":["0","1","2","−1"],"a":0,"e":"f(1) = 1 − 2 + 1 = 0, so (x−1) is a factor and the remainder is 0."},
   {"q":"Which of the following is a factor of x³ − 4x?","o":["x + 2","x + 4","x − 3","x + 1"],"a":0,"e":"x³ − 4x = x(x² − 4) = x(x−2)(x+2), so (x+2) is a factor."}]],
 ["Partial Fractions", ["partial fraction","denominator","numerator","proper","decomposition","linear"],
  "Partial fractions split a complicated rational expression into simpler fractions. For distinct linear factors, A/(x−a) + B/(x−b); for repeated factors, A/(x−a) + B/(x−a)². Solve for A and B by clearing denominators and substituting the roots. The decomposition is possible when the fraction is proper (degree of numerator less than degree of denominator).",
  [{"q":"Express 5/((x−1)(x−4)) as partial fractions: A/(x−1) + B/(x−4). Find A.","o":["−5/3","5/3","3/5","−3/5"],"a":0,"e":"5 = A(x−4) + B(x−1); putting x=1 gives 5 = A(−3), so A = −5/3."},{"q":"Partial fractions are used mainly to…","o":["simplify complex fractions","find roots only","solve triangles","expand brackets"],"a":0,"e":"They decompose a fraction into simpler parts for integration and algebra."}]],
 ["Binomial Expansion", ["binomial","coefficient","expansion","Pascal","term","nCr"],
  "The binomial theorem expands (a + b)ⁿ: the terms follow aⁿ, aⁿ⁻¹b, … with coefficients from Pascal's triangle or nCr. The general term is nCr × aⁿ⁻ʳ × bʳ. For n a positive integer the expansion has n+1 terms. Common exam tasks are finding a particular term or coefficient, or using the expansion with small values for approximations.",
  [{"q":"In the expansion of (1 + x)⁶, the coefficient of x³ is…","o":["6","15","20","10"],"a":2,"e":"6C3 = 20."},
   {"q":"The number of terms in the expansion of (a + b)⁸ is…","o":["8","9","7","10"],"a":1,"e":"A binomial to power n has n + 1 = 9 terms."}]],
 ["Permutations & Combinations", ["permutation","combination","arrangement","selection","nPr","nCr"],
  "Permutations count arrangements where order matters: nPr = n!/(n−r)!. Combinations count selections where order does not matter: nCr = n!/(r!(n−r)!). Distinct or repeated objects and restrictions (e.g., vowels together) change the count. Key question: does order matter? If yes, permute; if no, combine.",
  [{"q":"How many ways can 3 students be arranged in a row from 5?","o":["10","60","20","125"],"a":1,"e":"5P3 = 5×4×3 = 60 arrangements."},
   {"q":"How many committees of 2 can be chosen from 5 people?","o":["20","10","25","5"],"a":1,"e":"Order does not matter: 5C2 = 10."}]],
 ["Complex Numbers", ["complex","imaginary","i squared","real part","conjugate","modulus"],
  "A complex number is z = a + bi, where i² = −1; a is the real part and b the imaginary part. Operations follow normal algebra with i² replaced by −1. The conjugate of a + bi is a − bi, used to divide complex numbers by multiplying numerator and denominator by the conjugate. The modulus is |z| = √(a² + b²).",
  [{"q":"If z = 3 + 4i, then |z| = …","o":["7","5","25","12"],"a":1,"e":"|z| = √(9 + 16) = 5."},
   {"q":"The conjugate of 2 − 5i is…","o":["2 + 5i","−2 + 5i","−2 − 5i","5 − 2i"],"a":0,"e":"The conjugate changes the sign of the imaginary part."}]],
 ["Differentiation & Its Applications", ["derivative","gradient","tangent","stationary","maximum","rate of change"],
  "Differentiation finds the rate of change: if y = xⁿ, dy/dx = nxⁿ⁻¹. The derivative is the gradient of the tangent at a point; setting dy/dx = 0 locates stationary points, classified as maxima, minima or points of inflection by the sign of the derivative (or the second derivative). Rates of change in physics (velocity = ds/dt) and marginal cost use the same idea.",
  [{"q":"If y = 3x² − 4x + 7, then dy/dx at x = 2 is…","o":["4","8","12","6"],"a":1,"e":"dy/dx = 6x − 4; at x = 2 this is 8."},
   {"q":"A curve has dy/dx = 2x − 6. Its minimum point is at x = …","o":["−6","3","6","−3"],"a":1,"e":"2x − 6 = 0 gives x = 3, where the gradient changes from negative to positive."}]]
],
"Christian Religious Studies": [
 ["The Life of Moses", ["Moses","Exodus","Passover","Red Sea","Sinai","covenant"],
  "God called Moses at the burning bush and sent him to free Israel from Egypt. After ten plagues, the Passover (the angel passing over homes marked with lamb's blood) preceded the Exodus; the Red Sea divided and Israel crossed dry. At Sinai God made a covenant and gave the Ten Commandments, and Israel became God's people under the Law.",
  [{"q":"At the burning bush, God revealed His name to Moses as…","o":["El Shaddai","I AM WHO I AM","Adonai","The Almighty"],"a":1,"e":"God said 'I AM WHO I AM' (Yahweh) — His covenant name."},
   {"q":"The event that marked Israel's deliverance from Egypt was…","o":["the flood","the Passover and exodus","the tower of Babel","the exile"],"a":1,"e":"The Passover angel and the crossing of the Red Sea delivered Israel from slavery."}]],
 ["Kingship in Israel: Saul, David & Solomon", ["Saul","David","Solomon","kingdom","anointed","wisdom"],
  "Israel demanded a king, and Samuel anointed Saul, who was rejected for disobedience. David, chosen for his faithfulness, killed Goliath, became king and received God's covenant of an everlasting dynasty. Solomon, his son, asked for wisdom and built the Temple, but his many wives and idolatry divided the kingdom after his death.",
  [{"q":"Who was the first king of Israel?","o":["David","Saul","Solomon","Samuel"],"a":1,"e":"Saul was anointed first, then rejected and replaced by David."},
   {"q":"Solomon asked God for…","o":["riches","long life","wisdom","victory in war"],"a":2,"e":"His request for wisdom pleased God, who also granted riches and honour."}]],
 ["The Prophets: Elijah & Amos", ["Elijah","prophet","Baal","Carmel","Amos","social justice"],
  "Elijah confronted Ahab and the prophets of Baal on Mount Carmel, proving the Lord is God, then fled to Horeb where God spoke in a still small voice. Amos, a shepherd from Tekoa, condemned social injustice — the rich selling the poor for silver, dishonest courts and empty religion — and called Israel to 'let justice roll down like waters'.",
  [{"q":"On Mount Carmel, Elijah challenged the prophets of…","o":["Asherah","Baal","Dagon","Molech"],"a":1,"e":"The confrontation with Baal's prophets proved Yahweh is the true God."},
   {"q":"Amos condemned the Israelites mainly for…","o":["cooking food","cheating the poor and unjust courts","worshipping in the temple","celebrating festivals"],"a":1,"e":"Amos preached justice: bribery, oppression of the poor and hollow religion."}]],
 ["The Ministry of Jesus: Miracles", ["miracle","healing","nature","faith","compassion","sign"],
  "Jesus' miracles are signs of the kingdom: healing the sick (the leper, the paralytic), casting out demons, and controlling nature (stilling the storm, feeding the five thousand, raising Lazarus). They show His divine authority and compassion, and call for faith. Each miracle teaches: faith over fear, compassion over ritual, and God's power in human need.",
  [{"q":"Jesus fed five thousand with…","o":["seven loaves","five loaves and two fish","bread from heaven","one loaf"],"a":1,"e":"Five loaves and two fish fed the crowd, with twelve baskets left over."},
   {"q":"The raising of Lazarus shows mainly Jesus' power over…","o":["the sea","death","disease only","sin only"],"a":1,"e":"The sign points to Jesus as the resurrection and the life."}]],
 ["The Parables of Jesus", ["parable","kingdom","sower","good samaritan","prodigal","lesson"],
  "A parable is an earthly story with a heavenly meaning. The Sower teaches hearing the word and the responses to it; the Good Samaritan defines the neighbour as anyone in need and love as practical; the Prodigal Son reveals God's forgiving love; the Lost Sheep shows joy over repentance; and the Talents teach faithful stewardship.",
  [{"q":"The Good Samaritan parable was told to answer the question…","o":["who is the Messiah?","who is my neighbour?","how to pray?","how to be rich?"],"a":1,"e":"Jesus answered the lawyer's question with the story of practical love."},
   {"q":"In the parable of the Sower, the seed on rocky ground represents…","o":["wealthy hearers","hearers who fall away when trouble comes","faithful hearers","non-believers only"],"a":1,"e":"Shallow soil = hearers who receive the word gladly but fall away in persecution."}]],
 ["The Early Church & Paul's Mission", ["Pentecost","Holy Spirit","Peter","Paul","mission","persecution"],
  "At Pentecost the Holy Spirit filled the apostles and Peter preached, and about three thousand were baptised: the church was born. The church shared possessions, worshipped and grew despite persecution — Stephen became the first martyr. Saul met the risen Christ on the Damascus road, became Paul, and took the gospel through Asia Minor and Europe, writing letters that became part of the New Testament.",
  [{"q":"The church was born at…","o":["the crucifixion","Pentecost","the ascension","the transfiguration"],"a":1,"e":"The Holy Spirit descended at Pentecost and Peter preached the first sermon."},
   {"q":"On the Damascus road, Saul…","o":["was arrested","met the risen Christ","baptised Peter","preached in Rome"],"a":1,"e":"The encounter with Christ converted Saul into Paul, apostle to the Gentiles."}]]
],
"Islamic Religious Studies": [
 ["The Prophet Muhammad (SAW): Makkah & Madinah", ["Muhammad","Makkah","Madinah","Hijrah","prophethood","Da'wah"],
  "The Prophet (SAW) was born in Makkah, his prophethood began at 40 with the first revelation at Cave Hira, and he called people to Tawhid despite persecution. In 622 CE he migrated (Hijrah) to Madinah, where he founded the first Muslim community and the Constitution of Madinah. His patience in Makkah and leadership in Madinah model both patience and community building.",
  [{"q":"The first revelation came to the Prophet (SAW) at…","o":["Mount Arafat","Cave Hira","the Ka'bah","the garden of Madinah"],"a":1,"e":"Angel Jibril brought the first verses of the Qur'an in Cave Hira."},
   {"q":"The migration of the Prophet to Madinah is called…","o":["Hajj","Hijrah","Ijtihad","Tawakkul"],"a":1,"e":"The Hijrah in 622 CE marked the start of the Islamic calendar."}]],
 ["Hadith & Sunnah", ["Hadith","Sunnah","isnad","authentic","example","source of law"],
  "Hadith records the sayings, actions and approvals of the Prophet (SAW); his practice is the Sunnah. A hadith has a chain of narrators (isnad) and a text (matn); scholars rank them authentic (sahih), good (hasan) or weak (da'if). The Sunnah is the second source of Islamic law after the Qur'an, explaining and applying it in daily life.",
  [{"q":"The chain of narrators of a hadith is called the…","o":["matn","isnad","tajwid","sunnah"],"a":1,"e":"Isnad is the chain; matn is the text."},
   {"q":"The second primary source of Islamic law is…","o":["consensus only","the Sunnah","ijtihad","custom"],"a":1,"e":"The Sunnah explains the Qur'an and is the second source of Shariah."}]],
 ["Worship: Salat & Zakat", ["salat","zakat","prayer","purification","obligation","wudu"],
  "Salat is the five daily prayers — Fajr, Zuhr, Asr, Maghrib and Isha — preceded by wudu (ablution); it keeps the believer mindful of Allah. Zakat is the obligatory annual alms of 2.5% of qualifying wealth given to the poor and eight categories named in the Qur'an; it purifies wealth and reduces inequality. Both are pillars of Islam: the second (salat) and the third (zakat).",
  [{"q":"How many obligatory prayers are there in a day?","o":["three","five","seven","four"],"a":1,"e":"Fajr, Zuhr, Asr, Maghrib and Isha are the five daily prayers."},
   {"q":"Zakat is paid on qualifying wealth at a rate of about…","o":["1%","2.5%","5%","10%"],"a":1,"e":"2.5% (1/40) of savings, gold, silver and livestock held for a year."}]],
 ["Fasting (Sawm) & Hajj", ["sawm","Ramadan","hajj","ihram","Ka'bah","Eid"],
  "Sawm is fasting from dawn (Fajr) to sunset (Maghrib) in the month of Ramadan: no food, drink or marital relations, with extra restraint in speech and conduct; it teaches taqwa (God-consciousness) and empathy for the poor. Hajj is the pilgrimage to Makkah in Dhul-Hijjah, in ihram, circling the Ka'bah and standing at Arafat — required once in a lifetime for those able.",
  [{"q":"Fasting in Ramadan is from…","o":["sunrise to sunset","dawn (Fajr) to sunset (Maghrib)","midday to midnight","sunset to dawn"],"a":1,"e":"The fast begins at dawn and ends with Maghrib."},
   {"q":"The central ritual of Hajj, standing at the plain of…","o":["Mina","Arafat","Muzdalifah","Safa"],"a":1,"e":"Wuquf at Arafat is the essence of Hajj."}]],
 ["Islamic Law: Shariah & Ethics", ["shariah","halal","haram","ibadah","mu'amalat","justice"],
  "Shariah is the Islamic way of life drawn from the Qur'an and Sunnah: acts of worship (ibadah) and social dealings (mu'amalat). Halal is lawful, haram unlawful; justice, honesty and mercy are central. Islamic ethics teach truthfulness, trustworthiness, kindness to parents, good neighbourhood and cleanliness, and forbid lying, cheating, gambling and usury.",
  [{"q":"In Islamic law, an act that is forbidden is…","o":["halal","haram","mustahab","mubah"],"a":1,"e":"Haram is prohibited; halal is permissible."},{"q":"The lawful and unlawful in Islamic law are called…","o":["halal and haram","sunni and shia","makkah and madinah","zakat and sawm"],"a":0,"e":"Halal is lawful; haram is forbidden."}]],
 ["Islamic History: The Caliphate & Civilization", ["caliph","Abu Bakr","Umar","scholars","trade","civilization"],
  "After the Prophet, the Rightly-Guided Caliphs (Abu Bakr, Umar, Uthman and Ali) led the community; Abu Bakr fought the wars of apostasy and collected the Qur'an, Umar established the treasury and the calendar, Uthman standardised the mushaf and Ali upheld justice. Islamic civilization then flourished: Baghdad and Cordoba became centres of the translation movement, mathematics (al-Khwarizmi), medicine and trade across three continents.",
  [{"q":"The first caliph after the Prophet was…","o":["Umar","Abu Bakr","Uthman","Ali"],"a":1,"e":"Abu Bakr (RA) was chosen at Saqifah and served as first caliph."},
   {"q":"The compilation of the Qur'an into one mushaf was standardised under…","o":["Abu Bakr","Umar","Uthman","Ali"],"a":2,"e":"Uthman (RA) sent the standard copies of the mushaf to the provinces."}]]
],
"Data Processing": [
 ["Word Processing & Documents", ["word","format","document","template","heading","print"],
  "Word processors create letters, reports and brochures. Skills: typing and editing; formatting fonts, paragraphs and pages; headings and styles; tables and images; headers, footers and page numbers; spell-check and grammar tools; and mail merge for bulk letters. Save work in the right format and use print preview before printing.",
  [{"q":"Which feature sends one letter to many addresses with personal details?","o":["spell check","mail merge","page break","table of contents"],"a":1,"e":"Mail merge combines a template with a data source for personalised bulk letters."},{"q":"Bullets and numbering are used in documents to…","o":["hide text","list items clearly","change paper size","delete words"],"a":1,"e":"Lists improve organisation and readability."}]],
 ["Spreadsheets & Charts", ["spreadsheet","cell","formula","SUM","chart","filter"],
  "Spreadsheets organise numbers in a grid of cells referenced by column and row (A1). Formulas begin with = and use functions (SUM, AVERAGE, IF); absolute references ($) keep a cell fixed; sorting and filtering reorder data; charts (column, line, pie) and conditional formatting reveal patterns. Spreadsheets are used for marks, budgets, inventories and simple payrolls.",
  [{"q":"Given marks in B2:B10, the formula to find the average is…","o":["=TOTAL(B2:B10)","=AVERAGE(B2:B10)","=MEAN(B2;B10)","AVG(B2:B10)"],"a":1,"e":"AVERAGE returns the mean of the range."},{"q":"A pie chart is best used to show…","o":["a trend over time","parts of a whole","a comparison between two bars only","raw numbers only"],"a":1,"e":"Pie charts show proportions of a whole."}]],
 ["Database Design", ["database","design","entity","key","normalisation","relationship"],
  "Database design starts with identifying entities and attributes, then building tables with a primary key and relationships (one-to-many) between them. Normalisation removes redundant data and update anomalies (1NF, 2NF, 3NF). A good design is consistent, avoids repeated data and is easy to query."
  ,[{"q":"The attribute that uniquely identifies each record in a table is the…","o":["foreign key","primary key","data type","index"],"a":1,"e":"A primary key uniquely identifies each row."},{"q":"A one-to-many relationship links…","o":["two tables","one table field to many records in another","two files","two queries only"],"a":1,"e":"One record in a table relates to many records in another."}]],
 ["Presentation & Multimedia", ["presentation","slide","design","animation","multimedia","audience"],
  "Presentation software (PowerPoint, Google Slides) builds slide shows with text, images, audio and video. Good slides: one idea per slide, short bullets, high-contrast text, consistent design, and smooth transitions without distracting animation. Always rehearse and save in the right format (PPTX) and prepare a PDF backup for incompatible machines.",
  [{"q":"How many ideas should a good slide carry?","o":["as many as possible","one main idea","only pictures","ten bullet points"],"a":1,"e":"One clear idea per slide keeps the audience focused."},{"q":"Transitions in a presentation are…","o":["the slide design","effects when changing slides","the font size","printed notes"],"a":1,"e":"Transitions animate the move from one slide to the next."}]],
 ["Internet & Web Technologies", ["internet","web","browser","search","URL","social media"],
  "The Internet is a global network; the Web is the document service on it. Browsers fetch pages via URLs over HTTP/HTTPS; search engines index the web, and advanced search (quotes, site:) improves results. Other services: email, file transfer, video calls and social media. Web safety includes passwords, privacy settings and verifying information before sharing.",
  [{"q":"A search engine works by…","o":["storing all pages locally","indexing web pages and matching queries","scanning your emails","downloading the web"],"a":1,"e":"Search engines crawl and index pages, then rank matches to queries."},{"q":"The protocol of the World Wide Web is…","o":["FTP","HTTP","SMTP","POP"],"a":1,"e":"HTTP(s) carries web pages to browsers."}]],
 ["Data Security & Cyber Ethics", ["security","cyber","password","phishing","privacy","law"],
  "Data security guards confidentiality, integrity and availability: strong unique passwords, two-factor authentication, encryption, firewalls, antivirus and backups. Cyber threats include phishing, malware, identity theft and ransomware. Cyber ethics: do not plagiarise, hack, spread false information or bully; respect copyright and privacy, and report abuse. Nigerian law (Cybercrime Act) punishes cyber offences.",
  [{"q":"A fake email that tricks you into revealing your password is…","o":["spam","phishing","firewall","cookie"],"a":1,"e":"Phishing impersonates trusted organisations to steal credentials."},{"q":"A program that locks your files and demands payment is…","o":["ransomware","firewall","cookie","antivirus"],"a":0,"e":"Ransomware encrypts files and demands a ransom."}]]
],
"Food & Nutrition": [
 ["Meal Planning & Menu Design", ["meal","menu","planning","budget","balance","family"],
  "Meal planning arranges the week's meals to meet nutritional needs, the family budget and available time. Good menus: variety of foods, all six nutrient groups, seasonal and cheap foods, and appeal through colour, texture and flavour. Plan around a food budget, use a shopping list, and rotate menus to avoid monotony while staying within the family income.",
  [{"q":"The first consideration when planning a family menu is…","o":["colour of dishes","nutritional needs and budget","imported foods","popularity only"],"a":1,"e":"The menu must meet nutrient needs within the family's money and time."},{"q":"A shopping list is written from the…","o":["meal plan","food advertisement","leftovers","recipe only"],"a":0,"e":"The menu determines what to buy, within budget."}]],
 ["Methods of Cooking", ["boiling","frying","steaming","roasting","baking","grilling"],
  "Cooking makes food digestible, safe and attractive. Methods: boiling (in water, moist heat), steaming (vapour), stewing (slow in liquid), frying (in oil — shallow or deep), roasting and grilling (dry heat), and baking (oven). Each affects nutrients: boiling loses water-soluble vitamins, frying adds fat, steaming preserves the most nutrients.",
  [{"q":"Which cooking method conserves the most water-soluble vitamins?","o":["deep frying","steaming","boiling in much water","roasting"],"a":1,"e":"Steaming exposes food to vapour, minimising vitamin loss."},{"q":"Cooking in hot oil is called…","o":["boiling","frying","steaming","grilling"],"a":1,"e":"Frying cooks food in fat, shallow or deep."}]],
 ["Food Preservation", ["preservation","drying","freezing","salting","canning","spoilage"],
  "Food preservation prevents spoilage by micro-organisms and enzymes. Methods: sun/air drying (removes moisture), salting and curing, smoking, refrigeration and freezing (slow microbe growth), boiling and canning (sterilisation), pickling in acid, and chemical preservatives. Choose a method by food type: grains by drying, meat by smoking/freezing, vegetables by freezing or canning.",
  [{"q":"Grains and legumes are best preserved by…","o":["canning","drying","freezing","pickling"],"a":1,"e":"Drying removes moisture so micro-organisms cannot grow."},{"q":"Food that has spoiled is usually…","o":["safer to eat","unsafe and must be discarded","fresher","cheaper"],"a":1,"e":"Spoiled food can cause food poisoning."}]],
 ["Kitchen Hygiene & Safety", ["hygiene","sanitisation","safety","accident","contamination","storage"],
  "Kitchen hygiene prevents food poisoning: wash hands before cooking, keep raw and cooked foods apart, clean surfaces and utensils, store food at correct temperatures and check expiry dates. Safety: knife and fire care, pot handles turned inward, no trailing cables, fire extinguisher at hand, and correct use of gas and electrical equipment.",
  [{"q":"To prevent cross-contamination, raw meat and cooked food should be…","o":["stored together","kept separate","frozen together","cooked together longer"],"a":1,"e":"Separate boards, containers and shelves keep raw and cooked foods apart."},{"q":"Food should be stored at the correct temperature mainly to…","o":["improve taste","slow microbial growth","increase weight","save space"],"a":1,"e":"Cold storage slows the microbes that spoil food."}]],
 ["Nutritional Needs of the Family", ["nutrition","pregnancy","childhood","adolescent","elderly","balanced"],
  "Nutrient needs change through life: infants need breast milk then weaning foods; children need protein, calcium and iron for growth; adolescents need more energy, protein and iron; pregnant and lactating women need extra iron, calcium, folic acid and energy; the elderly need fewer calories but more calcium, protein and fibre. Balanced meals adapt to each stage and to activity.",
  [{"q":"Which nutrient do pregnant women need in extra amounts to prevent anaemia?","o":["iron","sugar","salt","starch"],"a":0,"e":"Extra iron and folic acid prevent anaemia in pregnancy."},{"q":"Adolescents need extra of which nutrient for growth?","o":["salt","protein and iron","fat only","water only"],"a":1,"e":"Rapid growth demands protein, calcium and iron."}]],
 ["Food Service & Entertaining", ["table","service","etiquette","placesetting","guest","host"],
  "Table service styles: table service (waiter), self-service (buffet) and family service. Correct table setting: fork left, knife and spoon right, glass above the knife, napkin at the side. Etiquette covers seating the guest of honour, serving from the right, clearing from the left, and polite conversation. A good host plans the menu, table and timing in advance.",
  [{"q":"In a formal table setting, the fork is placed…","o":["on the right","on the left","above the plate","behind the glass"],"a":1,"e":"Fork on the left (four letters, left) — knife and spoon on the right."},{"q":"In a formal setting, the napkin is placed…","o":["inside the glass","beside or on the plate","on the floor","under the chair"],"a":1,"e":"A folded napkin sits at the place setting."}]]
],
"French": [
 ["Le Présent: Verbes Réguliers & Irréguliers", ["présent","conjugaison","-er","-ir","être","avoir"],
  "Au présent, les verbes en -er prennent -e, -es, -e, -ons, -ez, -ent (parler: je parle, nous parlons). Les verbes en -ir prennent -is, -is, -it, -issons, -issez, -issent (finir: je finis). Être (je suis), avoir (j'ai), aller (je vais) et faire (je fais) sont irréguliers et doivent être appris par cœur pour construire toutes les phrases du niveau SS1.",
  [{"q":"Choisissez la bonne forme: Nous ____ à l'école.","o":["va","allons","allez","vont"],"a":1,"e":"Avec nous, le verbe aller prend -ons: nous allons."},
   {"q":"Conjuguez: Vous (finir) vos devoirs.","o":["finissez","finis","finissons","finissent"],"a":0,"e":"Avec vous, les verbes en -ir prennent -issez: vous finissez."}]],
 ["Le Passé Composé", ["passé composé","auxiliaire","participe passé","avoir","être","accord"],
  "Le passé composé exprime une action terminée: auxiliaire avoir ou être + participe passé. Avec avoir, le participe ne s'accorde pas avec le sujet (j'ai parlé). Avec être (je suis allé), le participe s'accorde en genre et en nombre (elle est allée, ils sont allés). Les participes passés irréguliers: prendre→pris, voir→vu, faire→fait, écrire→écrit.",
  [{"q":"Choisissez: Elle ____ au marché hier.","o":["a allé","est allée","est allé","a allée"],"a":1,"e":"Aller se conjugue avec être et s'accorde: elle est allée."},
   {"q":"Le participe passé de 'avoir' est…","o":["eu","avé","euvoir","avait"],"a":0,"e":"J'ai eu: le participe passé d'avoir est eu."}]],
 ["Le Futur Simple", ["futur","infinitif","terminaisons","dema","prochain","verbe"],
  "Le futur simple se forme avec l'infinitif + -ai, -as, -a, -ons, -ez, -ont: je parlerai, tu parleras, il parlera, nous parlerons. Les verbes irréguliers changent de radical: être→ser-, avoir→aur-, aller→ir-, faire→fer-, venir→viendr-. Il exprime une action à venir: demain, je finirai mes devoirs.",
  [{"q":"Choisissez: Demain, nous ____ au match.","o":["irons","allons","ira","iront"],"a":0,"e":"Le futur d'aller avec nous: nous irons."},
   {"q":"La terminaison du futur avec 'je' est…","o":["-e","-ai","-ais","-a"],"a":1,"e":"Je parlerai: la terminaison est -ai."}]],
 ["Les Adjectifs & Pronoms Possessifs", ["possessif","mon","ma","mes","sa","accord"],
  "Les possessifs s'accordent avec le nom: mon livre, ma maison, mes amis; son/sa/ses, notre/nos, leur/leurs. Pour la possession, on utilise aussi 'de' (le livre de Paul). Devant une voyelle, ma devient mon (mon amie). Les pronoms possessifs (le mien, la tienne, les nôtres) remplacent le nom déjà mentionné.",
  [{"q":"Choisissez: Voici ____ école.","o":["mon","ma","mes","mien"],"a":1,"e":"École est féminin: ma école devient mon école devant voyelle."},{"q":"Choisissez: C'est ____ stylo.","o":["mon","ma","mes","mienne"],"a":0,"e":"Stylo est masculin: mon stylo."}]],
 ["L'Interrogation & La Négation", ["interrogation","négation","est-ce que","ne...pas","question","intonation"],
  "Trois façons de poser une question: l'intonation (Tu viens ?), est-ce que (Est-ce que tu viens ?), et l'inversion (Viens-tu ?). Avec inversion, les verbes en -er prennent un -t: Parle-t-il ? La négation s'écrit ne…pas / ne…jamais / ne…rien autour du verbe: je ne sais pas, il ne travaille jamais. 'Ne' s'élide devant une voyelle: je n'aime pas.",
  [{"q":"Choisissez la forme correcte: Il ____ regarde la télévision.","o":["n'","ne","pas","non"],"a":1,"e":"Devant une voyelle, ne devient n': il ne regarde pas."},{"q":"Choisissez la forme correcte: ____ tu viens ?","o":["Est-ce que","Est-ce","Qu'est-ce","Que"],"a":0,"e":"Est-ce que tu viens ? est la question polie."}]],
 ["La Compréhension & L'Expression Écrite", ["compréhension","lecture","dialogue","expression","lettre","résumé"],
  "Pour la compréhension: lire le texte deux fois, repérer les mots-clés, identifier qui/quoi/où/quand et répondre avec des phrases complètes. Pour l'expression écrite: suivre la consigne (longueur et type de texte), soigner les accords, les temps et la ponctuation, et présenter proprement (salutation, corps, formule de politesse pour une lettre). Relire avant de rendre.",
  [{"q":"Avant de répondre à une question de compréhension, il faut…","o":["écrire vite","lire le texte attentivement","copier le texte","ignorer la consigne"],"a":1,"e":"Lire deux fois et repérer les mots-clés donne des réponses exactes."},{"q":"Dans une lettre, la formule de politesse se place…","o":["au début","à la fin","au milieu","dans le titre"],"a":1,"e":"La formule de politesse clôt la lettre."}]
]]
};
